{"name":"Red Beech","hex":"#7b3801","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"red","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The color of the setting sun, blood, and strawberries; the color which is evoked by the longest visible wavelengths (between about 625–740 nm), and a primary additive color.","A revolutionary socialist or (most commonly) a Communist;  a Bolshevik, a supporter of the Bolsheviks in the Russian Civil War."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a red hue.","Having an orange-brown or orange-blond colour; ginger."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/red"},{"term":"beech","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A tree of the genus Fagus having a smooth, light grey trunk, oval, pointed leaves, and many branches.","The wood of the beech tree."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/beech"}],"entries":[{"title":"Red beech","blocks":[{"text":"Red beech is a common name applied to several species of trees:"},{"items":["Dillenia alata, native to Northern Australia and New Guinea","Nothofagus fusca, native to New Zealand","Protorhus longifolia, native to South Africa"]}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Red_beech","wikidata":"Q16859929"},{"title":"Beech","blocks":[{"text":"Beech is a genus of deciduous trees in the family Fagaceae, native to subtropical and temperate Eurasia and North America. There are 14 accepted species in two distinct subgenera, Englerianae Denk & G.W.Grimm and Fagus. The subgenus Englerianae is found only in East Asia, distinctive for its low branches, often made up of several major trunks with yellowish bark. The better known species of subgenus Fagus are native to Europe, western and eastern Asia and eastern North America."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1c/Beech-tree.JPG/330px-Beech-tree.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Beech","wikidata":"Q25403"},{"title":"Red","blocks":[{"text":"Red is the color at the long wavelength end of the visible spectrum of light, next to orange and opposite violet. It has a dominant wavelength of approximately 625–750 nanometers. It is a primary color in the RGB color model and a secondary color in the CMYK color model, and is the complementary color of cyan. Reds range from the brilliant yellow-tinged scarlet and vermillion to bluish-red crimson, and vary in shade from the pale red pink to the dark red burgundy."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cf/Delicious_fresh_strawberries_%284735910403%29.jpg/330px-Delicious_fresh_strawberries_%284735910403%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Red","wikidata":"Q3142"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b5/367_Fagus_silvatica.jpg/120px-367_Fagus_silvatica.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b5/367_Fagus_silvatica.jpg/250px-367_Fagus_silvatica.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b5/367_Fagus_silvatica.jpg/120px-367_Fagus_silvatica.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3A367_Fagus_silvatica.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3f/Fagus_sylvatica_leaf_001.jpg/250px-Fagus_sylvatica_leaf_001.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3f/Fagus_sylvatica_leaf_001.jpg/500px-Fagus_sylvatica_leaf_001.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3f/Fagus_sylvatica_leaf_001.jpg/250px-Fagus_sylvatica_leaf_001.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipe